WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.202
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy Award-winning

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:06.906
hip-hop legend and member of
the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:10.410
His Rock the Bells Festival
will take place on Saturday,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.345
August 6th,
at Forest Hills Stadium

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.546
in Queens, New York.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:16.182
Tickets are on sale now
at rockthebells.com.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.651
Please welcome to the show
LL Cool J, everybody!

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:20.653
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:30.663 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.232 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:36.735
I am thrilled to have you here.

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:37.770
-Yo, what's up?

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.472
Giulliana, that was great.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.340
[ Laughter ]
Very cool.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.508
¿Cómo estás?
¿Todo bien?

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:45.311
-Todo bien.
-Bueno, bueno.

00:00:45.311 --> 00:00:46.879
-You're off
to a very charming start.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.948
[ Laughter ]
-You like that?

00:00:48.948 --> 00:00:50.983
-So wait, I -- you know,
obviously, I grew up with you.

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:52.885
This is a huge deal
to have you here.

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:56.022
But also, you've had this
incredible second act

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.624
in your career as an actor,
14 seasons now of...

00:00:58.624 --> 00:00:59.892
-Yeah, yeah.
-..."NCIS: Los Angeles."

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:01.194
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.596
-Thank you, thank you.
Thank you, thank you.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.832
-For me, you know,
it's impossible to believe

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:07.967
people wouldn't know you
as a rapper first,

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.636
but do you get recognized more
for the TV show now?

00:01:09.636 --> 00:01:11.838
-Oh, I get recognized for both,
but definitely,

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:13.272
there are a lots of people
who only know me

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:15.208
from either television,
from hosting,

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:16.876
from doing different things.

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.512
You know, quite frankly,
that's the joy of, you know,

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:20.780
some of the things I'm doing.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.483
It'll give people an opportunity
to see me, you know,

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.151
when I'm embracing
my first love.

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:27.286
You know what I'm saying?
But, yeah, a lot of people

00:01:27.286 --> 00:01:28.588
know me
for a lot of different things,

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:29.688
and that's a blessing, right?

00:01:29.688 --> 00:01:31.223
Like, I'm very thankful
for that.

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:32.959
And it's been a lot of fun.
You know what I'm saying?

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.462
But music is my first love.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:37.330
And, you know,
for those who don't know,

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.599
I guess we'll just have to
kind of show them.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:40.600
-Yeah.
-You know what I'm saying?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.602
[ Laughter ]
-It was --

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.370
[ Cheers and applause ]
I mean, this is --

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.238
This is an incredible bill
you have for Rock the Bells.

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:47.573
What was the idea --

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.642
Did you think that this was
an important thing to do?

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:52.312
-Yeah, the reason why I did this
is because

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.914
I feel like hip-hop culture
isn't disposable.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:58.851
I feel like the same deference
and love and respect

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:01.387
that the Beatles get
and that Paul McCartney gets

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.989
and Bob Dylan,
all those greats get, Madonna,

00:02:03.989 --> 00:02:07.593
I feel like, you know,
the Rakim and the Nas's

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:10.797
and the -- and the --
you know, the Run DMCs,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:13.566
and those artists also deserve
that kind of deference.

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.002
So, when we put this bill
together, you know, myself,

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:20.306
Ice Cube, Rick Ross,
you know, Dipset, Jadakiss,

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.007
I wanted --
what I wanted to do is say,

00:02:22.007 --> 00:02:24.810
"Hey, these kings and queens
deserve to be elevated.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.212
We need to celebrate
this culture,

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:27.646
and somebody has to do it."

00:02:27.646 --> 00:02:29.682
You know,
there's nothing wrong with,

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:31.183
you know,
serving things in a brown bag.

00:02:31.183 --> 00:02:32.285
That can be good.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.820
But sometimes
we want a silver platter.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.020
-Yeah, exactly.
-You know what I'm saying?

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.723
-Yeah.
-And so that's what this is.

00:02:36.723 --> 00:02:40.260
[ Cheers and applause ]
-And this was --

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.328
This must have been
quite an honor.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.764
There's a statue of you
in Corona Park now...

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:46.499
-Yeah.
-...where you grew up.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.834
I gotta say,
it's an outstanding --

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:51.537
the front of it is --
is really something to see.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:53.106
Were you --
-Yeah. Sherwin, the artist --

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:55.642
Sherwin, the artist, did it.
It's -- it's astounding.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:57.676
To me, like,
this is more about the culture

00:02:57.676 --> 00:02:59.112
than it is me, though.
-Yeah.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.081
-The reality is that, you know,
I was blessed to be a part

00:03:02.081 --> 00:03:04.717
of something bigger than me,
which is hip-hop culture.

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:07.252
You know, and I was
a generation one fan,

00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:09.755
a generation 1.5 artist, right?

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:11.257
So, I was the first artist --

00:03:11.257 --> 00:03:13.292
you know, first generation
to grow up on hip-hop,

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:17.096
but then, as an artist,
you know, I was, like, 1.5.

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.198
And, you know, it's --
what it's done is

00:03:19.198 --> 00:03:21.333
changed the lives of people
all over the world.

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:23.669
And it's been the underpinnings
of American pop culture

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:24.938
for many years, for the last --

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:26.839
it's gonna be 50 years old
next year.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:30.443
So, hip hop has really been
amazing to me.

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:33.079
And that's just
a testament to how --

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:35.247
how deeply
it's penetrated our culture.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:36.349
You know what I'm saying?

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.917
So, I don't really
take that to heart,

00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:39.451
like -- [ Stately voice ]
"I've got a statue."

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:40.753
[ Laughter ]
You know what I mean?

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.488
It's like -- you know.
-Yeah.

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.856
[ Both laugh ]
-You know what I mean?

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.157
-It doesn't look like that
kind of statue, I will say.

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:46.492
-Yeah. You know what I mean?
You know what I mean.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.760
-If you were on a horse
or something.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:48.961
[ Laughter ]

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:51.263
[ Imitating horse ]
-You know.

00:03:51.263 --> 00:03:52.665
-You know,
what's amazing to me --

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:54.500
I will say --
so, "Mama Said Knock You Out"...

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:55.535
-Yeah.
-...that's, like, only

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:57.670
five years after
your first album.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.472
-Yeah, something like that.
-Yeah.

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:00.839
-It's something like that, yeah.
Possibly the truth.

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:02.508
-It seems weird that even at,
like, only five years,

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:03.709
you were already mad at people

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:04.843
that they were calling it
a comeback.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:05.845
-[ Laughs ]
Yeah.

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:07.046
Well -- Well, you know what?

00:04:07.046 --> 00:04:08.614
You hit on an important point.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:11.617
You know, hip hop
is such a young genre that,

00:04:11.617 --> 00:04:14.687
for the most part, it's so young
that a lot of people expect

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:17.390
artists to come and go
and come and go and come and go.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:19.359
"Oh, here today, gone tomorrow."

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:21.227
They're not used to Santana.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:24.096
Like, we haven't had our Santana
moment in hip hop yet.

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:25.298
-Yeah.
-There are a lot of firsts

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:26.966
that haven't happened
in hip hop yet.

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:30.202
So, that's just part of
the kind of --

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:32.538
the growing pains
of a young genre.

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:34.640
But that's when you require
leadership, right?

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:36.775
When you have those holes,
when you have that vacuum.

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:38.511
That's why I stepped in
and said, "You know what?

00:04:38.511 --> 00:04:40.513
It looks like it's getting
a little off the rails,

00:04:40.513 --> 00:04:41.647
and we're getting to a point

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:43.449
where we're about to be
commoditized."

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:46.052
I love the younger generation.
There's no problems with it.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:48.821
I'm not one of those guys who
doesn't appreciate new art.

00:04:48.821 --> 00:04:51.590
I love all art
from all generations.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.626
I do think, though, there is
room for the roots of a culture

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:57.029
to be celebrated and lifted up
so that younger

00:04:57.029 --> 00:04:59.632
and newer generations understand
where it comes from

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:02.434
so that they can really embrace
the craft in a great way.

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.303
You know what I'm saying?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:04.303 --> 00:05:07.005
-Well, the other thing
I will say -- So true.

00:05:07.005 --> 00:05:09.308
I feel like the younger
generation of artists,

00:05:09.308 --> 00:05:11.810
you always -- you can hear
the previous generation

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.112
in the songs, right?

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:14.947
I feel like there's
a lot of students of the genre

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.882
who are making the music,
but I sometimes think people

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:18.985
who listen to it don't realize,
like, "Oh, that's from that,

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:20.419
and that's from that."
-That's true, that's true.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:21.987
-It must have been cool --
You were inducted into

00:05:21.987 --> 00:05:23.389
the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.891
That must've been a nice moment
to be like, "Hey, look,

00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:26.692
they're finally coming around
that this is

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:27.659
a really important piece
of music, as well."

00:05:27.659 --> 00:05:29.195
-I mean,
it was an amazing feeling.

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:30.996
I mean, I'm looking out
in the crowd, and I'm --

00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:33.099
I'm performing, I said,
"Yo, that's Paul McCartney there

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:34.199
with his phone out."
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:35.368
-You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:36.635
And I'm -- I'm rocking like,
"Look at Paul.

00:05:36.635 --> 00:05:38.303
He got his phone out.
This is crazy."

00:05:38.303 --> 00:05:40.172
Like, I really was thinking
that in my mind.

00:05:40.172 --> 00:05:42.408
Like, I'm giving you some
real insight right now.

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:44.310
"That's Paul McCartney
with his phone out."

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:46.645
Like, I'm really bugging out
off of that, right?

00:05:46.645 --> 00:05:48.114
-Yeah.
-It was amazing.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:52.018
It's an amazing feeling.
You know, I finally got in.

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:53.486
It was a beautiful feeling.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:54.787
-Did you choose
who got to induct you?

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:55.921
Was that a choice?
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:58.023
I personally asked
Dr. Dre to induct me.

00:05:58.023 --> 00:05:59.358
He's one of my, you know,
best friends in music.

00:05:59.358 --> 00:06:00.359
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:00.359 --> 00:06:01.860
-Absolutely, absolutely.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.664
And I asked J.Lo and Eminem
to join me and perform.

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:06.432
And, you know,
it was an amazing feeling

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:09.601
to just be up there
and to be embraced by your peers

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:10.937
at that level, you know,

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:13.706
which is arguably one of
the highest levels in music.

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.007
You know what I'm saying?
-I was really --

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.742
It was something to see,
and as a fan of the genre,

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:18.444
you know, I --
you know, you hope like,

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:20.546
"Oh, I hope it's now.
The dam's gonna break,

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:21.680
and we're gonna see
more and more artists

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:22.714
from your generation."
-We will.

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:23.882
-Because I think
you're right, too.

00:06:23.882 --> 00:06:25.084
I have a lot more to ask
about the festival.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:28.054
We'll be right back
with more from LL Cool J.

